    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:b9ad3e14216c2f08",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "3a8669cab849e7707600e70e5fe5f7271d21a7798abbeb9abd5738e37a3db1d5",
      "text": "Day 6  ·  Breakfast at the hotel. Private full-day excursion to Torres del Paine National Park with an English-speaking guide. Rising almost vertically above the Patagonian steppe, the granite spires of the Towers of Paine dominate one of South America’s finest national parks, created in 1959 on former sheep pastures and still recovering from almost a century of grazing, logging and poaching. Beyond the towers lie azure lakes, trails through emerald forests, wild rivers, swaying bridges and a great shining blue glacier.\n\nPart of UNESCO’s Biosphere Reserve network since 1978, the park is home to rheas (ñandú), Andean condors, flamingos and many other birds, and above all to the guanaco, whose growing, well-protected herds barely notice visitors. The day includes the Milodón Cave on Cerro Benítez, 25 km from Puerto Natales, where the remains of a sabre-toothed cat, a native horse and a giant ground sloth, the Mylodon darwinii, which lived here 8,000 to 10,000 years ago, were found in 1895; a life-size sculpture stands at the entrance. In the park, a 15-minute walk leads to Salto Grande, linking Lakes Nordenskjöld and Pehoé, and the return follows the Serrano River and Lakes Toro and Porteño. Overnight at the hotel.",
